>>>  I was playing the other day, on a unix system, and I had gotten nuked
>>>three or four times in a row on d.level five or six.  The last game I played
>>>when I got to d.level 5 there were THREE ghosts of myself on that level.
>>>  Unfortunately I was killed before I could make off with all that loot.
>>
>>>Is it me or is this unusual.
>>
>>>k.
>>
>>I've had this happen several times, mostly when there is a Real Nasty Critter
>>on the level (demon or something which wandered thru a polymorph trap).  This
>>is especially bad when the Bad Guys are clustered around the stairs leading
>>to the level; poor adventurers don't even get a move before they are pounded
>>by 4-8 demons, old pets, and ghosts.
>>
>>The heroic method of fixing this is to carefully build up a character on the
>>first few levels who can take on the Bad Guys.  Note that killing several
>>demons with a low level character is a true test of Nethacking ability.
>>
>>The wimpy method is to have a character enter that level and then Quit.
>>This will wipe out the bones level.
>>
>>The sneaky method is to get the correct privs and "rm" the file containing
>>the bones level :-).
>>
>>Anyone have any other ways to get past "killer bones levels"?
>>
>>     -Kevin
>>
>Hmmm... a level teleport will often do it... but is very risky.
>Another way is to dig a trapdoor from one level up and hope you land in a
>spot far enough away from any monsters to either a) hit the stairs down
>or b) dig another trapdoor to the next level.

If I remember, the is only a 1 in 4 chance of a bones level being created.
But the law of averages does not seem to be in effect when this occurs, does
it?  I've had the same thing happen to me.  Before you know it there are 4
ghosts walking around plus the original toughy or group of toughies who
started the mess.  Perhaps the rule on bones levels changes when one already
exists.  Anybody got proof?

Anyway, my solution is usually to just run away.  Head for the stairs.




